# Copy to .env before `docker compose up` (.env is gitignored):
#   copy .env.example .env
#
# Local dev SQL container only. The SA password must satisfy SQL Server's complexity rules
# (8+ chars, three of: upper / lower / digit / symbol) or the container fails to start.
#
# The value below is a FIXTURE, not a secret, and it is checked in on purpose — decided
# 2026-07-17, when the security audit asked why a plaintext password was sitting in a public
# repo. What it guards is a dev container restored from database/backup/CMS.bak, whose
# contents are test data; the container is rebuilt from that backup on demand. So the
# credential guards nothing worth guarding, and replacing it with a placeholder would cost
# every developer a manual step while un-publishing nothing: the repo is public and the value
# is already in the history, which only a force-push could change.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE REUSING THE PATTERN. "It's only local" is NOT why this is safe, and saying
# so would be false: docker-compose.yml publishes the port with no bind address, so the
# container listens on 0.0.0.0 and is reachable from the LAN and from any VPN/tailnet this
# machine is on — verified 2026-07-17, not theoretical. The contents being test data is the
# ONLY thing making that acceptable. If real data ever lands in this container, this decision
# is void: bind the port to 127.0.0.1 and stop treating this value as a fixture.
#
# The literal is copied elsewhere (spec/docker-db.md, and the http-docker profile in
# src/CMS.API/Properties/launchSettings.json, which cannot expand ${...}). Do not trust a
# count written here — `git grep LocalDev#Cms2026` before changing it, and change every hit.
#
# Nothing that guards anything real may join them. docker-compose.yml reads this variable and
# refuses to start without it, so a password that matters goes in .env, which is gitignored.
MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD=LocalDev#Cms2026

# Host port for the container. The native SQLEXPRESS uses a dynamic port, so 1433 is normally free.
SQL_PORT=1433
